multi-select.content:
Usage



MultiSelect is controlled: pass the array from onSelectionChange back to
selectedValues. The callback is the recommended API because it fires once per
user gesture with the complete resulting selection. Existing selection order is
retained, and newly selected filtered values are appended in item order.

Migrating from onChange

Replace toggle reducers or debounce logic attached to onChange with direct
array assignment through onSelectionChange. The legacy onChange callback
remains supported with its existing per-item string payloads and callback counts.

Server-side search

Pass searchText to make search controlled. In this mode, MultiSelect renders
that value, calls onSearchChange for every input change, and does not filter
items; supply the already-filtered API results yourself. Debouncing, request
cancellation, caching, and error handling remain consumer responsibilities.



Controlled mode is selected by the presence of searchText, including an empty
string. Omitting onSearchChange creates a read-only search input. Setting
enableSearch={false} hides the input but still disables internal filtering.
isSearchLoading describes search requests only; pagination continues to use
hasMore and loadingComponent.

The controlled query persists when the menu closes and reopens. Clear it in
your own lifecycle handler only when that is the experience you want, for
example with onOpenChange={(open) => !open && setQuery('')}. With remote
results, Select All applies only to the items currently supplied to the
component; it does not select every match on the server.

| Legacy MultiSelect prop | MultiSelectV2 equivalent |
| ------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| searchText              | search.searchText        |
| onSearchChange          | search.onSearchChange    |
| isSearchLoading         | search.isSearchLoading   |
| emptyStateText          | search.emptyStateText    |

